.TH CHTTP_RUN_QUERY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttp_run_query \- execute a one-shot request with explicit headers via the default client
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttp_run_query(chttp_method_t method, const char *url,
                              const chttp_request_body_t *body,
                              chmap headers,
                              chttpcli_response **resp_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Convenience wrapper: constructs a chttp_request_t internally, attaches
headers to it without taking ownership, calls chttp_do, then frees the
internal request object.  Unlike chttp_get/chttp_post/etc., this is the
one default-client convenience function that lets the caller supply an
arbitrary header set in one call, without building a chttp_request_t by
hand via chttp_request_new plus repeated chttp_request_set_header calls.

The caller retains full ownership of headers; it is only borrowed for
the duration of this call and is never freed or modified by it.

headers may use any casing for its keys (e.g. "Host", "Content-Type",
"Accept"): every key this module auto-injects a default value for
(host, accept, user-agent, content-length, content-type, authorization,
expect) is matched against headers case-insensitively, so a caller-
supplied header with natural casing correctly suppresses the matching
auto-injected default instead of appearing alongside it on the wire.

Because a chmap key is matched by exact byte content, headers may
legally contain two literally different keys that are the same header
name under HTTP's own case-insensitive semantics (e.g. "Host" and
"host" both present at once); unlike chttp_request_set_header, which
lower-cases every key before storing it and so can never produce this
situation, a directly-built headers map has no such guarantee. Only
one of the case-variant duplicates reaches the wire, never both:
whichever one was inserted into headers most recently wins, matching
how a single exact-key update already behaves; the other is silently
dropped rather than sent as a second, duplicate header line.

Because headers is handed to this function directly, bypassing
chttp_request_set_header's own validation, no key or value in it may
contain an embedded CR or LF byte (same rule and same rationale as
chttp_request_set_header: it would otherwise let CRLF-bearing data
inject extra header lines or split the request); such a headers map is
rejected outright with ccol_invalid_args, before any connection is
opened. headers must likewise not contain a "Transfer-Encoding" key
(any casing), for the same reason chttp_request_set_header itself
rejects one: this client never transfer-codes a request body, so
honoring one is impossible, and letting it reach the wire would
produce a request with both a caller-declared Transfer-Encoding and
this module's own auto-synthesized Content-Length, an ambiguous
framing over a body that was never actually transfer-coded.

A "Content-Length" key IS accepted in headers, but, on a
body-carrying request (POST, PUT, or PATCH), is rejected with
ccol_invalid_args if its value is not a plain unsigned decimal digit
string (no leading '+'/'-', no embedded whitespace) exactly matching
the actual body length being sent, once the request reaches the wire; see
chttpclient_do(3) and chttp_request_set_header(3) for the identical
check and the same "declared framing disagrees with the wire"
reasoning.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
method     HTTP method.
url        Target URL; must not be NULL.
body       Request body, or NULL for a bodyless request.
headers    chmap(char* -> char*) of request headers, or NULL for none.
           Borrowed for the duration of the call; not consumed or
           freed. No key or value may contain a CR or LF byte, and no
           key may be "Transfer-Encoding".
resp_out   Must not be NULL. Set to NULL immediately, then, on
           success, receives the response pointer.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Same codes as chttpclient_do, or ccol_not_enough_memory if the internal
request object could not be allocated. ccol_invalid_args if url or
resp_out is NULL, or headers contains a key or value with an embedded
CR or LF byte, or a "Transfer-Encoding" key.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttp_do (3),
.BR chttp_get (3),
.BR chttp_post (3),
.BR chttp_request_new (3),
.BR chttp_request_set_header (3),
.BR chttpclient_do (3)
